## [ROUND 1] CODEX — REVIEW

Scope checked: proposal lines in `proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md`, draft fragments in
`proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json`, frozen v0.4 prose/schema/reference/examples, `DECISIONS.md`
D001-D031, `docs/history/CHANGES.log`, and `research/findings/json-ld-event-platforms.md`. I also tried
`gh issue view 34/35/36 --repo openTechEvents/opentechevents-spec`; all three failed in this environment
with `error connecting to api.github.com`, so no finding below depends on issue-body-only evidence.

### P035

- **MAJOR — iCalendar loss table is too strong and therefore misleading.** The proposal says "No native term"
  and falls straight to `X-OTE-SPEAKER`/`DESCRIPTION` (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:164`). That is true for
  core RFC 5545 `VEVENT`, but not for current iCalendar-as-standardized: RFC 9073 explicitly updates RFC 5545
  (`RFC 9073:27-29`) and defines `PARTICIPANT` plus `PARTICIPANT-TYPE:SPEAKER` for event publication
  (`RFC 9073:187-193`, `RFC 9073` §6.2 / §7.1; the registered value is `SPEAKER`). v0.4 already admits
  post-5545 iCalendar extensions when useful (`spec/v0.4/README.md:300-303` discusses RFC 7986 `NAME`, and
  `proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:300` itself cites RFC 7986 for recordings), so the table should not present
  custom `X-OTE-SPEAKER` as the only honest export. Recommendation: say "RFC 5545 core has no native speaker;
  enhanced iCalendar MAY emit RFC 9073 `PARTICIPANT` with `PARTICIPANT-TYPE:SPEAKER`; otherwise degrade to
  `X-OTE-SPEAKER` or `DESCRIPTION`." Keep `ATTENDEE` forbidden; that part is correct.

- **MINOR — schema.org mapping should be narrowed to `url`, not casual `sameAs`.** The proposal maps
  `speakers[].url` to "`sameAs` or `url`" (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:163`). The field description says
  the URL may be "their own site, or their profile on the platform they publish from"
  (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:107-111`), matching `organizers[].url` (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:2538-2547`).
  schema.org `performer` expects `Person` or `Organization` and its own page confirms that presenter is in
  scope; the ordinary property for that page is `url`. `sameAs` is stronger identity-link semantics and not
  justified for an arbitrary event-platform profile. Recommendation: make `url -> url` normative, with `sameAs`
  only as an exporter MAY when the producer knows the page is an identity profile.

- **MINOR — order semantics are undefined for a list whose destination fallbacks are order-sensitive text.**
  `speakers` is an array with `uniqueItems` (`proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:35-40`), but the prose never
  says whether order is significant. v0.4 says order explicitly for `organizers`, because only the first one
  survives to some destinations (`spec/v0.4/README.md:407-410`), and `image` is also explicitly preference-ordered
  (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:153-155`). Speaker order matters for "keynote, panelist, moderator" display and
  for any text fallback. Recommendation: define the array as display/preference order, while still stating that
  distinct entries are not deduplicated by inferred identity.

Bottom line for P035: **into v0.5 with changes**: fix the iCalendar table to mention RFC 9073, tighten
schema.org URL mapping, and define list order.

### P036

- **BLOCKER — it does not clear the spec's own field-admission bar yet.** The proposal admits that none of the
  five studied JSON-LD producers emits a recording/video property (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:213-227`);
  the research file supports that the studied corpus covers Meetup, Eventbrite, Luma, Guild and Google's sample,
  with `performer` only in Google's music sample (`research/findings/json-ld-event-platforms.md:1-40`,
  `:102-153`, `:177-228`). The v0.4 extension rule is deliberately harsh: fields grow from "somebody already
  really uses" them, not imagined need (`spec/v0.4/README.md:809-824`). `cfp` is the exception because the
  value answers an OTE-native, time-sensitive discovery question and is actively scraped by directories
  (`spec/v0.4/README.md:751-753`). Recordings are useful archive links, but the proposal's own evidence is
  "communities upload videos" plus hand curation, not a structured producer and not a live OTE discovery filter.
  Recommendation: keep `recordings` out of v0.5; document it as an unprefixed extension candidate and require
  at least one real OTE/community feed to ship it before graduation.

- **MAJOR — the RSS/Atom mapping is wrong for the proposed value shape.** The draft explicitly allows a watch
  page URL and says it need not be the media file itself (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:260-263`), but the
  mapping table proposes `<media:content medium="video" url="...">` (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:301`).
  Media RSS says `media:content@url` should be the direct URL to the media object and that a player page belongs
  in `media:player` when no direct media URL is supplied (Media RSS 1.5.1 lines 115-133 and 231-237). A YouTube
  watch URL in `media:content@url` therefore overstates what OTE knows. Recommendation: map watch pages to an
  item/body link, or to `media:player url` when choosing Media RSS; only use `media:content` if a future field
  explicitly carries a direct media URL and type.

- **MAJOR — `recordingEntry.title` introduces free text without the translation machinery every comparable
  nested text field has.** The draft says `title` is "Free text, in the document's textLanguage"
  (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:266-268`; schema fragment `:96-101`), but there is no
  `recordingEntry.translations`, no `recordingTranslations` def, and no update to the top-level "any
  translations map requires `textLanguage`" trigger. v0.4 handles every comparable nested free-text field with
  local translations: `image[].alt` (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:2883-2910`), `offers[].name`
  (`:3028-3038`), `partOf.name` (`:2622-2632`), and `eligibility.note` (`:2813-2823`), with D010/D030 policing
  those maps (`spec/v0.4/DECISIONS.md:211-223`, `:603-617`). Recommendation: either remove `title` until there
  is evidence for labelled multi-recording entries, or add a local `translations` map and wire it into the same
  D010/D030 constraints and generated reference.

- **MAJOR — `https`-only contradicts the v0.4 URL-field direction without enough evidence.** The proposal
  requires `^https://` for both bare and object recording URLs (`proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:75-93`)
  because "every video platform is https" (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:274-275`). v0.4/P034 deliberately
  moved URL-like fields to HTTP(S) IRIs and even relaxed images to `http://` or `https://`
  (`docs/history/CHANGES.log:2863-2869`; current URL fields use `^https?://`, e.g.
  `spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:127-132`, `:2538-2544`, `:2983-2989`). A community self-hosted archive over
  plain HTTP is not less plausible than an image over HTTP, and no back-compat corpus exists because the field
  does not exist. Recommendation: reuse the shared HTTP(S) IRI rule unless the proposal brings real evidence
  that accepting `http` recordings creates false data rather than just less ideal links.

- **MINOR — semantic duplicates survive the string-or-object form.** `uniqueItems` catches only exact JSON
  duplicates (`proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:68-80`), so `["https://video.example/talk",
  {"url":"https://video.example/talk","title":"Full session"}]` is valid while naming the same recording twice.
  D024/D027 intentionally limit duplicate rejection to exact repeats (`spec/v0.4/DECISIONS.md:541-551`), so this
  is not a contradiction, but the proposal's "No exact duplicates" checklist item should not be oversold as
  deduplication. Recommendation: document that consumers may still need URL-level dedupe, or avoid object form
  until title/translations are justified.

Bottom line for P036: **stay out / stay an extension**. Reason: producer evidence is still below the
`cfp`-exception bar, and the draft mapping/translation/URL rules need redesign before standardization.

### P037

- **BLOCKER — Path A is not compatibility-neutral; it changes the meaning of existing v0.4-shaped documents.**
  v0.4 says `offers` describes the price/registration "of each document" and that the single registration of a
  multi-part event is not modelled (`spec/v0.4/README.md:237-258`, `:721`). It also says absent `offers` means
  unknown, never free (`spec/v0.4/README.md:688-695`; reference default rules at `spec/v0.4/reference.en.md:11`
  and `:59`). Path A says that when a parent OTE document exists with `offers`, parts that omit `offers` inherit
  the parent's price and consumers MUST read it that way (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:366-374`). That is
  not "prose only"; it is a new contextual default for an existing absent field. Any already-valid v0.4 feed
  that happens to publish a parent event with `offers` plus child parts without `offers` changes from "part price
  unknown" to "part price equals parent price" under a v0.5 consumer. Recommendation: do not make Path A a MUST
  inheritance rule. At most say consumers MAY display a resolvable parent's `offers` as context, but MUST NOT
  materialize it as the part's own `offers` unless v0.5 introduces an explicit wire signal.

- **BLOCKER — Path B reuses `$defs/offers` but misses the translation constraints required by D010/D030.**
  Adding `partOf.properties.offers` exactly as the fragment proposes (`proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:26-31`)
  creates a new location where `offers[].translations` can appear. The existing event-level condition that makes
  `textLanguage` required enumerates `translations`, `eligibility.translations`, `partOf.translations`,
  top-level `offers[].translations`, and `image[].translations` only (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:18-93`);
  D010's custom keyword scope is the same set (`spec/v0.4/DECISIONS.md:211-223`). I verified this with Ajv by
  adding `partOf.offers` in-memory to the v0.4 schema: a standalone event with
  `partOf.offers: [{price:45,currency:"EUR",translations:{en:{name:"General"}}}]` and no `textLanguage`
  validated `true` with `errors: null`. Recommendation: if Path B survives, update every translation-related
  rule and recommended-profile rule that currently knows about `offers[].translations`: base `textLanguage`
  requirement, `distinctTranslationLanguages`, D030 primary-text warning for `offers[].name`, generated
  reference paths, and invalid examples.

- **MAJOR — Path B puts ticket data inside an identity/reference object and weakens the "consumer can ignore
  `partOf` safely" contract.** v0.4 defines `partOf` as "A REFERENCE, never a recurrence rule" and says a
  consumer ignoring it still sees complete, correct events (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:984-986`); the prose
  says `partOf` only groups occurrences and degrades harmlessly because ignored identity/context leaves
  incomplete data, not false data (`spec/v0.4/README.md:237-270`). `offers`, however, is actionable event
  economics: price, registration URL, availability, waitlist and sale windows (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:921-923`,
  `:2951-3061`). Putting it inside `partOf` makes the reference object carry operational registration state,
  and a consumer that ignores `partOf` now misses the price for a paid part. Recommendation: if v0.5 wants a
  self-contained signal, prefer an explicit event-level field such as `sharedOffers`/`parentOffers` or an
  `offersScope` discriminator over hiding actionable data inside the reference. That third option avoids Path
  A's semantic inheritance and Path B's "reference object grows a ticketing submodel" problem, though it still
  needs real producer evidence.

- **MAJOR — the contradiction rules for duplicated `partOf.offers` are underspecified.** Path B's own cost
  section says if two parts disagree on `partOf.offers`, the answer is "undefined, publisher error"
  (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:398-402`). That is much weaker than the current intra-document consistency
  decisions: duplicate event IDs, self-`partOf`, inverted dates/windows, and feed/event `updatedAt` are validator
  errors where the contradiction is structurally checkable (D011/D012/D004/D009/D015). A feed-root check could
  compare exact `partOf.offers` blocks for parts sharing the same `partOf.id`, the same way D011 checks IDs at
  feed root (`spec/v0.4/DECISIONS.md:227-241`) and D015 checks event/update relations (`:297-309`). Recommendation:
  either keep the field out, or define whether exact disagreement across sibling parts is a base error,
  recommended warning, or deliberately unvalidated; "undefined" is not enough for a field whose main cost is
  redundancy.

- **MAJOR — Path A's "paid conference always has a parent page and will publish it" is an unsupported premise.**
  `partOf.id` explicitly does not have to resolve to an OTE document (`spec/v0.4/README.md:251`;
  `spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:2579-2588`), and the only valid multipart example publishes three parts but no
  parent event document (`spec/v0.4/examples/feed-multipart.json:14-102`). The proposal's claim that the limit
  is acceptable because a paid conference "will publish" the parent (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:376-380`)
  is therefore a hypothesis, not evidence. Recommendation: mark this as an open gap unless a real paid
  multipart producer demonstrates that parent OTE documents are published consistently.

- **MINOR — reused `offers` constraints are mostly correct but need explicit examples at the new site.**
  Path B correctly reuses `$defs/offers`, so non-zero `price` still requires `currency`, `currency` without
  `price` is still invalid, `waitlistUrl` with `availability: "in-stock"` is still invalid, and inverted
  sale windows remain invalid (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:2943-3100`; `spec/v0.4/README.md:698-715`). But
  because the new site is nested under `partOf`, failures will be harder to spot and generator/reference code
  has historically missed deeper nested recommended paths (D030 implementation notes at
  `docs/history/CHANGES.log:2854-2860`). Recommendation: add invalid examples for
  `partOf.offers[].currency` without `price`, positive `price` without `currency`,
  `waitlistUrl` + `in-stock`, and inverted `opensAt`/`closesAt`.

Bottom line for P037: **stay out / stay an extension** for v0.5 as currently framed. If it moves forward, it
needs changes: reject Path A as normative inheritance, fix Path B's translation/recommended/generator coverage,
define cross-part contradiction handling, and consider an explicit event-level scope signal instead of nesting
actionable ticket data inside `partOf`. The honest verdict today is still "open gap".

