## [ROUND 2] CODEX — REVIEW

Scope checked: current `proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md`, current `proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json`, and
the full log above. I re-checked the Round-1 evidence against v0.4 schema/prose where the revised text touches
rules (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json`, `spec/v0.4/event.recommended.schema.json`, `spec/v0.4/README.md`) and
against RFC 9073 for the iCalendar speaker mapping.

### P035

- **MAJOR — RFC 9073 is now cited, but the concrete `PARTICIPANT` mapping is still not valid enough.** The
  revised row correctly says RFC 5545 core has no speaker term and enhanced iCalendar MAY use RFC 9073
  (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:164`), and the component/property/value names are broadly right:
  `PARTICIPANT`, `PARTICIPANT-TYPE`, `SPEAKER`. But the row says "`CN` from `name` and `URL` from `url`"
  (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:164`). RFC 9073's `PARTICIPANT` component grammar requires
  `participanttype / uid` exactly once (`RFC 9073:713-723`), and its optional properties include `summary` and
  `url` (`RFC 9073:724-737`). `CN` is not the participant's display-name property in that component. The
  examples use `UID`, `PARTICIPANT-TYPE`, `STRUCTURED-DATA`, `DESCRIPTION`, nested `VLOCATION`, etc., not
  `CN` (`RFC 9073:748-792`). Therefore an OTE exporter cannot just emit name+URL as written; it must also
  provide a stable participant `UID`, and should map `speakers[].name` to `SUMMARY` (or `DESCRIPTION` as a
  weaker fallback) rather than `CN`. Recommendation: rewrite the iCal mapping as: enhanced iCalendar MAY emit
  one `PARTICIPANT` per speaker with required `PARTICIPANT-TYPE:SPEAKER`, required stable `UID` minted from a
  stable speaker URL when present or otherwise from the event id plus a stable representation of the speaker
  entry, `SUMMARY` from `name`, and `URL` from `url` when present. If the proposal does not want to specify UID
  minting yet, keep RFC 9073 as an optional advanced export and keep `X-OTE-SPEAKER`/`DESCRIPTION` as the
  normative fallback.

- **No new objection — schema.org `url` mapping and speaker list order are fixed.** The revised schema.org row
  makes `url -> url` normative and leaves `sameAs` as a MAY only when the exporter knows the URL is an identity
  profile (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:163`). The `$defs/speakers` description now defines
  display/preference order and exact-repeat-only uniqueness (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:90-97`;
  `proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:4-13`, `:35-40`), matching the v0.4 pattern for `organizers` and
  `image`.

Bottom line for P035: **ROUND 2 DOES NOT CONVERGE**. The only remaining open objection is the RFC 9073 export
details: replace `CN` with `SUMMARY`/`URL`, and account for required `PARTICIPANT.UID`. With that fixed, the
proposal remains **into v0.5 with changes**.

### P036

- **NIT — one stale word remains in the non-normative draft.** The prose still says an entry is "a bare
  `https` IRI string" (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:238-240`), while the actual fragment now correctly
  allows HTTP(S) (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:252-260`; `proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:68-80`,
  `:88-94`). Recommendation: change that phrase to "a bare HTTP(S) IRI string" if this draft is kept as future
  reference.

- **No new objection — the Round-1 fixes are correct for future adoption.** The verdict is now explicitly
  "does NOT get into v0.5" and "stays an unprefixed extension candidate" (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:228-234`),
  which resolves the admission-bar blocker. The draft `recordingEntry` translation wiring mirrors the
  v0.4 `imageTranslations` pattern structurally: parent `dependentRequired: { translations: [title] }`
  (`proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:84-109`) mirrors `imageEntry`'s dependency
  (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:2866-2910`); `recordingTranslations` uses `type: object`, `allOf` with
  `$defs/languageMap`, and `additionalProperties` to per-language entries (`proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:110-115`),
  matching `imageTranslations` (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:2912-2923`); each `recordingTranslation` requires
  `title` and validates non-whitespace text (`proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:116-127`), matching the
  required translated-field shape used by `imageTranslation` (`spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:2924-2942`).
  The proposal also correctly states that actual adoption must update the fixed translation-rule sites
  (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:292-296`; `proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:26-31`), and the RSS
  mapping now avoids `media:content` for watch pages (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:318-324`).

Bottom line for P036: **ROUND 2 CONVERGES**. The field should **stay out / stay an extension**. Only the stale
"https" wording should be cleaned up if the draft remains as future reference; it does not reopen the verdict.

### P037

- **No new objection — rejecting Path A as a MUST is the right compatibility call.** The revised proposal now
  states the original MUST would create a new contextual default for absent `offers` and conflict with v0.4's
  "absent means unknown" rule (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:390-405`; v0.4 evidence at
  `spec/v0.4/README.md:688-695` and `spec/v0.4/reference.en.md:59`). Keeping only the MAY-context-display
  form is compatible because it does not materialize parent offers as the part's own offers
  (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:402-405`).

- **No new objection — Path B is now framed honestly as possible but smelly/incomplete.** The revised text
  carries forward the required translation-rule updates for `partOf.offers[].translations`
  (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:423-435`; `proposals/v0.5-schema-fragments.json:26-31`), the safely-ignorable
  `partOf` design conflict (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:436-440`; v0.4 contract at
  `spec/v0.4/event.schema.json:984-986`), and the unresolved cross-part contradiction question
  (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:441-446`). That is the right state for a path explicitly not recommended.

- **No new objection — Path C is a sound tracking option, not a v0.5 solution.** The event-level
  `parentOffers`/`offersScope` family avoids Path A's retroactive inheritance and Path B's nesting of
  actionable registration data inside `partOf` (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:448-458`). It still needs real
  producer evidence and the same translation-rule work if it carries offer translations, which the proposal
  states. I do not see a materially better fourth option today: "just copy the parent ticket into top-level
  `offers`" is already v0.4-valid but keeps the central ambiguity from `proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:364-366`;
  an `offers[].scope` variant is just Path C pushed one level down; feed-level inheritance is already rejected
  for the right reason (`proposals/v0.5-event-fields.md:493-494`).

Bottom line for P037: **ROUND 2 CONVERGES**. The defensible verdict remains **stay out / stay an extension** /
open gap: no schema/prose rule should enter v0.5 beyond, at most, the compatible MAY-context-display note.

